---
import ChildrensLayout from '@/layouts/ChildrensLayout.astro';
---

<ChildrensLayout
  title="Honoring Our Guides"
  subPage="honoring-our-guides"
  eyebrow="Children&rsquo;s Theatre"
  description="Acknowledging the ancestors, teachers, artists, and children whose work shapes DT:FC&rsquo;s Children&rsquo;s Theatre."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-8">
    <p>
      We are grateful perpetually for those who came before us as we evolve our training and
      decades of experience into an international Developmental Theatre: Fearless Creativity
      platform.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Oral-tradition ancestors</h2>
      <p>
        We honor our ancestors around the world who packed survival knowledge into stories they
        told down the generations. As the oral tradition faded into print we honor the
        storytellers, poets, read-aloud parents, and children&rsquo;s book publishers who kept
        the stories alive.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Fred Rogers</h2>
      <p>
        We honor Fred Rogers for his <em>Mister Rogers&rsquo; Neighborhood</em> television
        program, where he taught children about the Land of Make Believe and closed each show
        with &ldquo;I love you&hellip; just the way you are.&rdquo;
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Chuck and Lola Wilcox</h2>
      <p>
        We honor Chuck and Lola Wilcox for fearlessly creating the Colorado Caravan&rsquo;s
        first children&rsquo;s play as part of the worldwide oral tradition. We honor the actors
        who toured that play and brought back the first pictures and stories. The Wilcoxes and
        graduates of the Colorado Caravan went on to write multiple other plays in this
        old&ndash;new tradition.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The teachers who first told these stories</h2>
      <p>
        The original design for the Colorado Caravan was to provide Colorado Shakespeare Festival
        actors work touring a Shakespeare program to schools and other venues. Even before the
        program launched, requests arrived for a play suited to elementary schools. We honor the
        administrators, principals, and teachers who made it possible for thousands of students
        to participate &mdash; and all those who later created theatre companies, theatre
        departments, and programs that carried this tradition forward.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>The children themselves</h2>
      <p>
        Each year the teachers sent the children&rsquo;s thank-you notes and hundreds of
        pictures &mdash; usually around twenty per classroom. We honor those children whose
        pictures showed us what they were hearing and imagining. They fostered the knowledge
        base, and every play in this library has been shaped by their honesty.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</ChildrensLayout>
